{"_data": [{"contents": [{"type": "lesson_name", "content": ""}, {"type": "unit_name", "content": "Tools to Collect Information"}, {"type": "position", "content": 12}, {"type": "origin", "content": "stub"}, {"type": "lesson", "content": 2}, {"type": "level", "content": "A1.1"}], "type": "metadata"}, {"contents": [{"type": "test", "content": "false"}], "type": "achievment_test"}, {"contents": [{"type": "text", "content": "These are words you use to talk about tools to collect information."}], "type": "target_word_describer"}, {"contents": [{"type": "primary", "content": "Gives information about instruments for data collection. Apply methods for data collection, designing the necessary instruments for information processing, according to the situation set by the company."}, {"type": "secondary_1", "content": "Understands key information in written texts about the communicative topics related to this level.    Recognizes basic ideas on IT concepts, systems theory, and diagrams of processes."}], "type": "objectives"}, {"type": "background_image", "content": "enpe8n1le02ob12re1img01.jpg"}, {"contents": [{"type": "heading", "content": "Simple Present Review All Forms"}, {"type": "describer", "content": "The <strong>Simple Present</strong> tense is used to express habits, routines and general ideas."}, {"type": "text", "content": "<strong>Structure:</strong></br></br>  <strong>Subject</strong> + <strong> [VERB] </strong> +<strong> s/es</strong> (third person) </br></br>  1.\tThe simple present tense uses the base form of the verb. For example: <i>I <strong>work</strong> in Bogota.</i> </br> 2.\tHowever, the third person (she/he/it) adds an -s: <i>She work<strong>s</strong> in Bogota.</i> </br> 3.\tFor the negative and the interrogative form you can used auxiliary verb DO/DOES and the main uses the base form. For instance: <i><strong>Do</strong> you work in Bogota? No, I <strong>don't</strong> work. </br></br></i>  Use: </br> 1. You can used the Simple Present to talk about a repeated or usual action. </br></br>  E.g.: <i>I work in a school.</i> </br></br>  2. It can also designate the orator thinks or knows that a fact was true before, is true now, and will be true in the future or to make generalizations about people or things. </br></br>  E.g.: <i>The sun rises every morning.</i>"}, {"type": "table", "content": "{\"rows\":8,\"columns\":3,\"cells\":[\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"<div class=\\\"center_text\\\"><span class=\\\"strong\\\">Affirmative</span></div>\\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"<div class=\\\"center_text\\\"><span class=\\\"strong\\\">Negative</span></div>\\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"<div class=\\\"center_text\\\"><span class=\\\"strong\\\">Interrogative</span></div>\\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"I work\\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"I don't work\\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"Do I work?\\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"You work\\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"You don't work\\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"Do you work\\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"He works\\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"He doesn't work\\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"Does he work?\\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"She works\\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"She doesn't work\\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"Does she work?\\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"It works\\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"It doesn't work\\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"Does it work?\\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"We work\\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"We don't work\\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"Do we work?\\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"They work\\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"They don't work\\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"Do they work?\\\"}\"],\"styles\":\" intro_table_border instruction_table_background\"}"}, {"type": "example", "content": "These tools extract data on variables from an established individual, group or situation."}, {"type": "example", "content": "Some people don't behave naturally when someone else observe them."}], "type": "learning_points"}, {"contents": [{"type": "heading", "content": "How to Write a Short Paragraph"}, {"type": "describer", "content": "When you want to do a written document, you need to know how to organize your ideas in paragraphs. It will help you to guide your reader throughout the whole text and have a well-structured paper.  "}, {"type": "text", "content": "To write a short paragraph, you always need to plan your writing, clarify your ideas, and define the structure of your paper. Make sure your text includes the following parts: </br></br>  <strong>Parts of a paragraph: </strong></br> \u2022\tMain idea: This is a sentence or phrase that summarizes the subject of your writing. </br> \u2022\tSupporting ideas: These are ideas that permit to argument your text. These could be facts, figures, examples, statistics, etc. </br> \u2022\tConclusion: At the end, it is advisable to write a conclusion or short summary of your main idea. This will help your reader to understand the point of your paragraph. </br></br>  <strong>Recommendations: </strong></br> 1. Use capital letter at the beginning of the sentence. </br> 2. Use linking words. These give your text cohesion and coherence. "}], "type": "learning_points"}, {"contents": [{"type": "name", "content": "research"}, {"type": "definition", "content": "an investigation of certain topic "}, {"type": "part_speech", "con